-- Leg 1 of the HRIS pipeline: BambooHR -> Postgres.
--
-- Pulls employee records out of a real HRIS and lands them in the same Neon Postgres the
-- Instant Teams platform already reads. That closes a real gap: today the platform reads a
-- Paylocity SIMULATOR, so it has never been pointed at a live HR system. This points it at
-- one without changing a line of workflow code — the landing table is mapped into the
-- `employees` table the app already reads in LEDGER_MODE=pg, by
-- striim/map_bamboohr_to_employees.sql.
--
-- PROPERTY NAMES WERE NOT GUESSED. The BambooHR Reader is in Preview and its properties are
-- not in the public docs, so they were discovered against the live cluster by creating the
-- source and reading back DESCRIBE. Verified working shape:
--
--   AuthenticationMode  'API token'
--   APIKey              the BambooHR API key   <- REQUIRED, stored encrypted
--   APIToken            also accepted; APIKey is the one that authenticates
--   Domain              the BambooHR subdomain, NOT a full URL  <- REQUIRED
--   Tables              semicolon-delimited
--   Mode                Automated | InitialLoad | IncrementalLoad   (exact casing)
--   MigrateSchema       true -> the reader creates the target table for you
--
-- Three things that cost time to find:
--   * Mode is case-sensitive and unspaced. 'Initial load' fails with
--     "No enum constant com.striim.app.source.enums.ReaderMode.Initial load".
--   * Domain is required and separate from any endpoint URL. Omitting it fails with
--     "Please provide a valid value for Domain."
--   * A dead BambooHR tenant does not fail as an auth error. An expired trial answers
--     HTTP 503 on the API while its portal still redirects, and the reader surfaces that as
--     "Table [Employees]: Test Connection failure. HTTP protocol error. 404 Not Found."
--     Check the tenant with a plain curl before believing the reader is misconfigured.
--
-- WHY ONLY `Employees` IS READ HERE — this was previously wrong in this file.
-- 'jobInfo;employmentStatus;compensation' appear in BambooHR's /meta/tables and were once
-- configured here on the theory that they carry effective-dated role, status and pay. They do
-- not arrive. The connector accepts them silently — no error, app stays RUNNING — and creates
-- no landing table for them. Verified: bamboohr_jobinfo, bamboohr_employmentstatus and
-- bamboohr_compensation do not exist in Postgres after a successful load. Leaving them
-- configured advertises a capability the pipeline does not have, so they are gone. The fields
-- they were meant to supply (hireDate, dateOfBirth, status, payRate) come from BambooHR's
-- custom-report endpoint instead — see scripts/enrich-bamboohr.ts.

USE __NS__;

CREATE APPLICATION BambooHRToPostgres;

CREATE SOURCE BambooEmployees USING Global.BambooHRReader (
  AuthenticationMode: 'API token',
  APIKey: '__BAMBOO_KEY__',
  APIToken: '__BAMBOO_KEY__',
  Domain: '__BAMBOO_DOMAIN__',
  -- The reader's `Employees` table is BambooHR's DIRECTORY: 20 columns of identity and
  -- contact detail. The directory omits inactive and terminated people — the new tenant
  -- returns 90 here against 109 in the custom report. That gap is the reason `status` has to
  -- come from the report, and it is also why a row's ABSENCE from this feed is not evidence
  -- that the person has left.
  Tables: 'Employees',
  -- Kept deliberately even though Tables is explicit: the reader is in Preview, and an
  -- earlier deployed copy of this app carried it. Cheap insurance against the reader
  -- widening what it enumerates in a future build.
  ExcludeTables: 'TimeOffRequests',
  -- Automated: bulk-read once, then keep polling for changes. THIS is what makes the demo
  -- work — InitialLoad reads once and stops, so a person added in BambooHR afterwards never
  -- arrives. Automated is the difference between a snapshot and a live feed.
  Mode: 'Automated',
  MigrateSchema: true,
  -- 5m, and NOT because 5m is good: the connector enforces a floor. '30s' is rejected at
  -- CREATE SOURCE time with "Please provide a valid value greater than 300s for Polling
  -- Interval." An earlier version of this file specified 30s with a comment claiming it was
  -- measured; it cannot have been, because the source will not create at that value. The
  -- deployed app running at 5m was therefore never configuration drift — it was the only
  -- value the reader accepts.
  --
  -- What this means for a demo, stated plainly: a person added in BambooHR can take up to
  -- five minutes to appear in bamboohr_employees. Do not wait on that in front of an
  -- audience. The enrich step reads BambooHR's custom-report endpoint directly and is bound
  -- by no such floor, so a new hire reaches the platform's `employees` table on the
  -- watcher's 30s cycle; Striim's landing table catches up behind it.
  PollingInterval: '5m'
)
OUTPUT TO BambooEmployeeStream;

CREATE TARGET BambooLanding USING DatabaseWriter (
  ConnectionURL: 'jdbc:postgresql://__HOST__/__DB__?sslmode=require&stringtype=unspecified',
  Username: '__USER__',
  Password: '__PASS__',
  -- Landing table, deliberately separate from the app's own `employees` table. Striim owns
  -- the shape here; a mapping step owns the app's contract. Mixing the two means every
  -- change in BambooHR's payload is a change to the application's schema.
  Tables: 'Employees,public.bamboohr_employees',
  CommitPolicy: 'EventCount:200,Interval:10',
  BatchPolicy: 'EventCount:200,Interval:10',
  CheckpointTable: 'CHKPOINT',
  IgnorableExceptionCode: 'DUPLICATE_ROW_EXISTS,NO_OP_UPDATE,NO_OP_PKUPDATE,NO_OP_DELETE',
  ConnectionRetryPolicy: 'retryInterval=30, maxRetries=3'
)
INPUT FROM BambooEmployeeStream;

END APPLICATION BambooHRToPostgres;
